                <pb facs="0300" n="228" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg228"/>

                <head>No. 118.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Lowell to. Mr.
                    Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-03-31"><hi rendition="#italic">March 31</hi>,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>[Mr. Lowell reports the conclusion arrived at by the British cabinet, which has
                    been put in the form of a memorandum by Lord Granville, as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>The communications which have been made by Mr. Lowell as to the
                            “suspects” in Ireland alleged to be American subjects involve principles
                            of such importance that Her Majesty’s Government consider it better to
                            give their views in a dispatch which I will soon address to Mr. West. In
                            the mean time I may say to Mr. Lowell that the imprisonment of the
                            suspects under the act of last year is not a measure of punishment, but
                            of prevention. * * *]</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
